Extension 3: ESLint

================================================================================

Purpose

ESLint analyzes JavaScript/React code using configured linting rules.

It can:

Detect code issues.
Flag configured bad practices.
Display warnings/errors.
Suggest or apply fixes for supported rules.
Help enforce coding standards.
Typical Workflow
Step | Action
-----|--------------------------------------------
1    | Install ESLint extension
2    | Open an ESLint-configured project
3    | ESLint analyzes supported files
4    | Problems can appear in the editor
5    | Review the issue
6    | Apply Quick Fix where supported

Examples of Issues React/ESLint Rules May Detect
Unused Variable
const unused = 5;

Hook Dependency Issue
useEffect(() => {
  console.log(count);
}, []);


Depending on the React Hooks lint configuration, count can be flagged as a missing dependency.

Missing React List Key
{items.map(item => (
  <li>{item}</li>
))}


Better:

{items.map(item => (
  <li key={item.id}>
    {item.name}
  </li>
))}

Benefits
Benefit             | Purpose
--------------------|--------------------------------------
Early Detection     | Identify problems during development
Rules               | Enforce configured coding standards
Quick Fix           | Fix supported issues faster
Consistency         | Helps teams follow common conventions


